basic_keyfile::get_locale_string() assigns a std::locale object to a
std::string when the current locale is invalid:

    localename = std::locale::classic();

That only compiles with libstdc++, which provides the non-standard implicit
conversion std::locale::operator std::string().  libc++ (used by the OHOS
clang toolchain) rejects it with "no viable overloaded '='" / "no known
conversion from 'const std::locale' to 'const std::string'".  Ask the locale
for its name explicitly, exactly as the success branch above already does.
No platform conditional is involved.

--- a/sbuild/sbuild-basic-keyfile.tcc
+++ b/sbuild/sbuild-basic-keyfile.tcc
@@ -214,7 +214,7 @@
     }
   catch (std::runtime_error const& e) // Invalid locale
     {
-      localename = std::locale::classic();
+      localename = std::locale::classic().name();
     }
   std::string::size_type pos;
   bool status = false;
